/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A flexible sensor sits on a backing that bends: the sensing film, electrodes and wiring are all deposited on it, so the device wraps around a wrist, a pipe or a food packet.
   - Four substrates cover most work.
   - Conductors are printed, not etched.
   - Encapsulation is not optional.
   - A printed strain gauge is a track of silver or carbon on the flexible film.
   - A crack-based gauge is a thin metal or carbon film on PDMS, deliberately precracked.
   - A capacitive pressure pad is two printed electrodes on either side of a foam or PDMS dielectric.
   - Printed silver or platinum tracks serve as RTDs, though a printed silver RTD drifts more than platinum wire and needs its own calibration; printed NTC paste with β of 3000 K to 4000 K serves as a thermistor.
   - Ratings to quote: minimum bend radius and fatigue.
   - One sensor has blind spots, drifts, and a single failure ruins the reading.
*/
