/* book exam traps: Averaging two readings 50/50 when their uncertainties differ. The weight is 1/σ², not 1/σ. | Forgetting that correlated errors survive averaging. Two sensors on the same board share self-heating and the same reference voltage, so their common offset is not reduced at all. | Mixing up standard deviation and variance in the weight formula, or dropping the unit of weight, which is the inverse square of the measured quantity. | Confusing bend radius with bend angle. A smaller radius is the harsher test; a device rated at 5 mm may fail at 1 mm after the same number of cycles. | Assuming a crack-based gauge is linear over its whole range. Its gauge factor is huge but the response saturates beyond about 1 % strain. */
